---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'syntax', title: 'Syntax' },
  { id: 'not-a-type', title: 'A mixin is not a type' },
  { id: 'order', title: 'Field order is the contract' },
  { id: 'diamond', title: 'Diamonds' },
  { id: 'collisions', title: 'Collisions' },
  { id: 'cycles', title: 'Cycles' },
  { id: 'docs-attributes', title: 'Doc comments and attributes' },
  { id: 'schema-lock', title: 'Schema lock and generated code' },
  { id: 'diagnostics', title: 'Diagnostics' },
];
---

<DocsLayout title="Mixins — IonPath" current="/guides/mixins/" toc={toc}>
  <div class="animate-fade-in-up">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-2">Mixins</h1>
    <p class="text-text-secondary mb-8">A <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">mixin</code> is a reusable field set. Every <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">msg</code> that names it in a <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">with</code> clause gets its fields spliced in at compile time. It is <em>not</em> a type, has no wire identity of its own, and generates no code.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-12 animate-fade-in-up-delay-1">

    <section>
      <h2 id="syntax" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Syntax</h2>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">mixin</span> <span class="type-name">Audited</span> {'{'}
    createdAt: <span class="type-keyword">datetime</span>;
    createdBy: <span class="type-keyword">guid</span>;
{'}'}

<span class="text-accent-3">mixin</span> <span class="type-name">Traced</span> <span class="text-accent-3">with</span> <span class="type-name">Audited</span> {'{'}
    traceId: <span class="type-keyword">guid</span>;
{'}'}

<span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Document</span> <span class="text-accent-3">with</span> <span class="type-name">Audited</span>, <span class="type-name">Traced</span> {'{'}
    title: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">A mixin's body is the same production as a <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">msg</code> body, so anything you can write in a message field you can write in a mixin field. A mixin may itself compose other mixins with its own <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">with</code> clause.</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3"><code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">with</code> is available on <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">msg</code> and on <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">mixin</code>, and on nothing else — a union, service, enum, flags or typedef has no field list to mix into. Once <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">with</code> is written at least one name is required; <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">msg M with { }</code> is a parse error, not a silently empty clause.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="not-a-type" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">A mixin is not a type</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">This is the whole design, and the one thing worth internalising. A mixin is a <strong>template for a field set</strong>, spliced into its includers at compile time. Concretely:</p>
      <ul class="text-sm text-text-secondary space-y-2 mt-3 ml-4">
        <li>• It <strong>cannot be written in type position</strong>. <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">owner: Audited;</code> is <a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0066" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0066</a>. The name resolves perfectly well — it is the position that rejects it.</li>
        <li>• It gets <strong>no entry in <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">ion.lock.json</code></strong>. The lock records <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Document</code> with four fields; <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Audited</code> and <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Traced</code> are not in the file at all.</li>
        <li>• It <strong>generates no declaration</strong> in any target. There is no C# <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Audited</code> class and no TypeScript <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Audited</code> interface.</li>
        <li>• It <strong>shares the declaration namespace</strong> with types. A <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">mixin Audited</code> beside a <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">msg Audited</code> is <a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0002" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0002</a>, because otherwise <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">with Audited</code> and <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">x: Audited</code> would name two different things.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-4">If you want a type, declare a <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">msg</code>. Mixins are for the case where several messages carry <em>the same fields inline</em>, not for the case where several messages carry <em>a reference to the same nested value</em>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="order" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Field order is the contract</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Ion messages are positional on the wire: a field's identity <em>is</em> its index. So the order expansion produces is the field numbering, and it is pinned:</p>
      <ol class="text-sm text-text-secondary space-y-2 mt-3 ml-4 list-decimal list-inside">
        <li>the mixins named in the <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">with</code> clause, left to right;</li>
        <li>within each one, its own expansion first — base mixins before their includer, transitively — then that mixin's own fields in declaration order;</li>
        <li>then the declaration's own fields, in declaration order.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-4">In other words: depth-first over the clause, left to right, base-first. Reading the clause left to right and each mixin top to bottom gives you the wire order. The example above produces exactly:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-text-muted">// msg Document with Audited, Traced {'{'} title: string; {'}'}</span>
<span class="text-text-muted">// index 0</span>  createdAt: <span class="type-keyword">datetime</span>   <span class="text-text-muted">// from Audited</span>
<span class="text-text-muted">// index 1</span>  createdBy: <span class="type-keyword">guid</span>       <span class="text-text-muted">// from Audited</span>
<span class="text-text-muted">// index 2</span>  traceId:   <span class="type-keyword">guid</span>       <span class="text-text-muted">// from Traced</span>
<span class="text-text-muted">// index 3</span>  title:     <span class="type-keyword">string</span>     <span class="text-text-muted">// declared on Document</span></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="glass rounded-xl p-4 mt-4 border-l-2 border-accent/40">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium mb-1">Why this rule and not a cleverer one</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary">Any rule that needed a linearisation algorithm to predict — C3, MRO, anything of that family — would mean an author could not tell what index their own field had without running the compiler. On a positional wire format that is not a usability wrinkle, it is a correctness hazard. The rule above is the one you can compute by reading.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="diamond" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Diamonds</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4"><strong>A mixin contributes once, however many paths reach it.</strong> Deduplication is <em>by mixin identity</em>, not by field name.</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">In the example, <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Document</code> reaches <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Audited</code> twice — directly, and through <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Traced</code> — and gets its two fields once. A mixin's fields land at its <strong>first</strong> position in the left-to-right transitive walk, which is what makes the order rule well defined:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">A</span> <span class="text-accent-3">with</span> <span class="type-name">Audited</span>, <span class="type-name">Traced</span> {'{'} x: <span class="type-keyword">i4</span>; {'}'}
<span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">B</span> <span class="text-accent-3">with</span> <span class="type-name">Traced</span>, <span class="type-name">Audited</span> {'{'} x: <span class="type-keyword">i4</span>; {'}'}

<span class="text-text-muted">// Both produce: createdAt, createdBy, traceId, x</span></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">The field numbering does not depend on the order the includer happened to list them in. In the second case <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Traced</code> is visited first, its base <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Audited</code> is spliced base-first, and the later <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Audited</code> entry then contributes nothing — it is redundant, and <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">with Traced</code> alone would give the same four fields.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="collisions" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Collisions</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">What survives the identity dedupe is a real conflict: two <em>different</em> mixins declaring the same field name, or a mixin field colliding with the declaration's own. Both are <a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0065" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0065</a> and both name their two sources, because neither is obviously the one to change and the other is usually in a different file.</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">mixin</span> <span class="type-name">Audited</span> {'{'} createdAt: <span class="type-keyword">datetime</span>; {'}'}

<span class="text-accent-3">msg</span> <span class="type-name">Doc</span> <span class="text-accent-3">with</span> <span class="type-name">Audited</span> {'{'}
    createdAt: <span class="type-keyword">string</span>;   <span class="text-text-muted">// ION0065 — Doc would have two fields called 'createdAt'</span>
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">Note that this is a <em>name</em> collision, not a type mismatch — two mixins contributing <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">id: u4</code> each still collide. There is no "same field, so merge them" rule, because merging would silently pick one of the two field <em>positions</em>, and position is identity.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="cycles" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Cycles</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">A mixin that transitively includes itself is <a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0064" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0064</a>, reported once per cycle with the whole chain printed. Unlike a type cycle (<a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0030" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0030</a>) there is no modifier that can break it: expansion is a compile-time splice, so a cycle has no fixed point at all. There is no <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">?</code> to add.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="docs-attributes" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Doc comments and attributes</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">A mixin field carries its <a href="/guides/comments/" class="text-accent hover:underline">doc comment</a> and its <a href="/guides/attributes/" class="text-accent hover:underline">attributes</a> into every message that includes it — the expansion reuses the very same field nodes the author wrote, so there is nothing to keep in sync:</p>
      <div class="code-block text-[13px]">
        <pre><span class="text-accent-3">mixin</span> <span class="type-name">Audited</span> {'{'}
    <span class="text-text-muted">/// When the record was first written.</span>
    <span class="text-accent-2">{'@'}deprecated</span>(<span class="text-accent-2">"2.0"</span>, <span class="text-accent-2">"use createdAtUtc"</span>)
    createdAt: <span class="type-keyword">datetime</span>;
{'}'}</pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">Every message including <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Audited</code> gets the doc comment on its generated member and the <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">[Obsolete]</code> / JSDoc / <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">#[deprecated]</code> emission that goes with it.</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-3">A doc comment or an attribute on the <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">mixin</code> declaration itself attaches to the mixin, which nothing generates — so it documents the template for readers of the schema and the editor, and goes no further.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="schema-lock" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Schema lock and generated code</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mb-4">Because a mixin has no wire identity, the lock sees only the expanded messages. That has a consequence worth stating plainly:</p>
      <div class="glass rounded-xl p-4 border-l-2 border-status-degraded/40">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium mb-1">Editing a mixin is editing every message that includes it</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-text-secondary">Adding a field to <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">Audited</code> appends it to the mixin's own field run — which is <em>ahead of</em> the fields of every includer — so it shifts the index of every subsequent field in every message that includes it. You get <a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0021" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0021</a> (field reordered) per shifted field per affected message, plus the usual <a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0029" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0029</a> for the new non-nullable field — and no diagnostic at all on the mixin, which is not in the lock. Adding a field to the <em>end</em> of a message that includes a mixin is still a safe append.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary mt-4">Generated code shows the expansion and nothing else. <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">msg Document with Audited, Traced</code> emits one C# record with four positional parameters and one TypeScript interface with four properties, with no trace of where the fields came from. There is no inheritance in the output; a mixin is a source-level convenience, not a runtime relationship.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="diagnostics" class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Diagnostics</h2>
      <div class="type-grid">
        <div class="type-grid-header">
          <span>Code</span>
          <span>Meaning</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px]"><a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0063" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0063</a></span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">A bad <code class="bg-oled-elevated px-1 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px]">with</code> clause entry: the name is undeclared, it names something that is not a mixin, or it is listed twice.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px]"><a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0064" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0064</a></span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">A mixin that transitively includes itself.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px]"><a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0065" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0065</a></span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">Two fields with the same name reaching one declaration.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px]"><a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION0066" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION0066</a></span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">A mixin written where a type is expected.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="type-grid-row">
          <span class="font-mono text-[12px]"><a href="/guides/diagnostics/#ION1001" class="text-accent hover:underline">ION1001</a></span>
          <span class="text-[12px] text-text-secondary">Info — a mixin that no message or mixin includes.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</DocsLayout>
